# What must never enter the build context, and why.
#
# The context is uploaded to the daemon before the first instruction runs, so
# anything listed here is a straight saving on every build. Three of these
# entries are correctness rather than speed:
#
#   node_modules/  — the host's tree is darwin/arm64 with native `.node` addons
#     (onnxruntime-node, sharp, @neplex/vectorizer). Copying it into a
#     linux/amd64 image produces a container that fails at the first require()
#     with an ELF-class error, which reads as a code bug rather than a packaging
#     one. The image installs its own from the lockfile instead.
#   .models/       — 130 MB of ONNX weights that the image *fetches itself* (see
#     the `weights` stage in the Dockerfile). Copying the host's copy would make
#     the image reproducible only on a machine that had already run the pipeline.
#   .env           — real API keys. They belong in the runtime environment, and
#     `hydrate.ts`'s own header says why a key in the environment of a long-lived
#     server is already the weaker of two options; baking one into a layer is
#     worse again, because layers get pushed.
#
# `projects/` is here for size (481 MB on the development machine, 33 MB per
# finished film) and because project directories are per-deployment state that
# lives on a volume, never in an image.

node_modules
**/node_modules

.git
.gitignore
.github

.next
**/.next
dist
**/dist
out
**/out
*.tsbuildinfo

.models
projects
renders
.inkcast
playwright-report
test-results
.pnpm-store

.env
.env.local
.env*.local
!.env.example

*.log
.DS_Store

# Deployment descriptors are read by the host, not by the image.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
docker-compose.yml
fly.toml
Caddyfile
